  "core_idea": "Un- turns a word into its opposite: not, or doing the opposite of an action so that something goes back to how it was.",
  "senses": [
    {
      "n": 1,
      "signpost": "not",
      "definition": "added to an adjective or an adverb, or to a form such as known or expected, to make a word meaning not that",
      "explanation": "Un- goes with everyday adjectives (unhappy, unkind, unable) and with forms ending in -ed or -ing (unknown, unfinished, unwilling). Many words from Latin take in-, im-, il-, or ir- instead (impossible, illegal, irregular), and some take dis- or non- (dishonest, nonstop); no rule says which, so learn each word. Not un- is a mild positive: not unhappy means fairly happy.",
